---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Sidebar from '../components/Sidebar.astro';

const sidebarGroups = {
  items: [
    { icon: 'help_outline', label: 'WHY_OSTK', href: '/why/' },
    { icon: 'rocket_launch', label: 'START_HERE', href: '/start/' },
    { icon: 'architecture', label: 'ARCHITECTURE', href: '/docs/architecture/' },
    { icon: 'memory', label: 'KERNEL_SPEC', href: '/docs/kernel-spec/' },
    { icon: 'shield', label: 'TRUST_MODEL', href: '/docs/trust-model/' },
    { icon: 'gavel', label: 'FIVE_LAWS', href: '/docs/five-laws/' },
  ],
  bottomItems: [
    { icon: 'menu_book', label: 'ALL_DOCS', href: '/docs/' },
    { icon: 'code', label: 'SOURCE', href: 'https://github.com/os-tack/ostk.ai' },
  ],
};
---

<Base title="Why ostk — the three problems it solves" description="You're using AI agents to code. Three things break: they collide, they drift, they leave no trace. ostk fixes all three from underneath, without changing how you drive your agents.">
  <Sidebar title="WHY_OSTK" subtitle="THE_PROBLEM" groups={sidebarGroups} />

  <main id="main-content" class="flex-1 md:ml-64 pt-16 pb-12 px-4 md:px-10">
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto py-12">
      <div class="section-label mb-4">DIAGNOSIS // 01</div>
      <h1 class="serif-text text-6xl font-bold tracking-tighter text-on-surface mb-6">Why ostk</h1>

      <p class="font-headline text-xl text-on-surface-variant italic leading-relaxed max-w-2xl mb-4">
        ostk is a modular local-first runtime that sits underneath whatever drives your agents. Three things break without it.
      </p>
      <p class="font-body text-sm text-secondary leading-relaxed max-w-2xl mb-16">
        You're using AI agents to code. ostk resolves the three core failure modes of autonomous coding using a decoupled, open-source stack.
      </p>

      <!-- Problem 1 -->
      <section id="collision" class="mb-16 scroll-mt-24">
        <div class="section-label mb-4">PROBLEM_01 // COLLISION</div>
        <h2 class="serif-text text-3xl font-medium text-on-surface mb-4">They collide.</h2>
        <div class="max-w-xl space-y-4">
          <p class="font-body text-sm text-secondary leading-relaxed">
            Two agents edit the same file. One finishes a second after the other. Whose version wins is whoever wrote last — silently overwriting files. The first agent's work is lost, and the system fails silently.
          </p>
          <p class="font-body text-sm text-secondary leading-relaxed">
            <strong class="text-on-surface">Modular Solution:</strong> The <code>ostk</code> daemon manages workspace paths using Optimistic Concurrency Control (OCC). Any agent write that conflicts with concurrent modifications gets a descriptive JSON-RPC error, letting the agent abort or run a merge conflict resolution handler automatically.
          </p>
          <p class="font-body text-xs text-outline">
            Handled by: <a href="/docs/ostk/" class="text-primary hover:underline">ostk daemon</a> & <a href="/docs/libostk/" class="text-primary hover:underline">libostk IPC client</a>.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Problem 2 -->
      <section id="drift" class="mb-16 scroll-mt-24">
        <div class="section-label mb-4">PROBLEM_02 // DRIFT</div>
        <h2 class="serif-text text-3xl font-medium text-on-surface mb-4">They drift.</h2>
        <div class="max-w-xl space-y-4">
          <p class="font-body text-sm text-secondary leading-relaxed">
            Each agent session runs in its own context window. They re-discover codebase details. They forget previous decisions. They don't see concurrent actions, forcing you to manually copy and paste context across chats.
          </p>
          <p class="font-body text-sm text-secondary leading-relaxed">
            <strong class="text-on-surface">Modular Solution:</strong> The <code>ostk-recall</code> memory MCP server watches the workspace via socket notifications, indexing changes incrementally to a local LanceDB vector store. Built-in tools let the agent retrieve real-time context without wasting context window or suffering drift.
          </p>
          <p class="font-body text-xs text-outline">
            Handled by: <a href="/docs/ostk-recall/" class="text-primary hover:underline">ostk-recall MCP server</a> with local embeddings.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Problem 3 -->
      <section id="trace" class="mb-16 scroll-mt-24">
        <div class="section-label mb-4">PROBLEM_03 // NO_TRACE</div>
        <h2 class="serif-text text-3xl font-medium text-on-surface mb-4">They leave no trace.</h2>
        <div class="max-w-xl space-y-4">
          <p class="font-body text-sm text-secondary leading-relaxed">
            Something went wrong. There's no auditable record of what prompt generated a command, what files were read, or who authorized the action. Security reviews stall because proprietary binary wrappers hide agent execution.
          </p>
          <p class="font-body text-sm text-secondary leading-relaxed">
            <strong class="text-on-surface">Modular Solution:</strong> Every action is logged to a structured, append-only Write-Ahead Log (WAL) that is cryptographically signed. Because our client integration layer — <code>libostk</code> — is **MIT licensed and fully open-source**, operators can audit the socket communication directly. There are no opaque binary wrappers: you get absolute visibility and total control.
          </p>
          <p class="font-body text-xs text-outline">
            Handled by: <a href="/docs/libostk/" class="text-primary hover:underline">libostk client library</a> & GPG signature chains.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- The shape -->
      <section class="mb-16">
        <h2 class="section-label mb-4">THE_SHAPE</h2>
        <p class="font-headline text-lg text-on-surface italic leading-relaxed max-w-xl mb-6">
          A decoupled, open-source stack. Adopt only what you need.
        </p>
        <div class="max-w-xl space-y-4 font-body text-sm text-secondary leading-relaxed">
          <p>
            ostk is built as a set of modular components. You don't have to adopt the full suite. You can drop in <code>ostk-cache</code> to save tokens immediately, connect <code>ostk-recall</code> as an MCP server to add memory to Cursor, or build custom integrations against the permissive MIT-licensed <code>libostk</code>.
          </p>
          <p>
            Choose your onboarding path:
          </p>
          <ul class="space-y-2 list-none pl-0">
            <li><span class="text-primary mr-2">→</span>Explore the modular architecture comparison on our <a href="/stack/" class="text-primary hover:underline">Stack Page</a>.</li>
            <li><span class="text-primary mr-2">→</span>Read how the userspace client links applications at <a href="/docs/libostk/" class="text-primary hover:underline">libostk Reference</a>.</li>
            <li><span class="text-primary mr-2">→</span>Install the full CLI daemon for complete integration via <a href="/start/" class="text-primary hover:underline">Quick Start</a>.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- What you give up -->
      <section class="mb-16">
        <h2 class="section-label mb-4">WHAT_YOU_GIVE_UP</h2>
        <p class="font-body text-sm text-secondary leading-relaxed max-w-xl mb-4">
          The trade-offs the rest of the page didn't lead with.
        </p>
        <div class="bg-surface-container-low p-6 border-l-2 border-outline max-w-xl space-y-3">
          <div class="font-body text-xs text-secondary">
            <strong class="text-on-surface">A learning curve.</strong> The vocabulary — <a href="/docs/humanfile/" class="text-primary hover:underline">humanfile</a>, <a href="/docs/agentfile/" class="text-primary hover:underline">agentfile</a>, <a href="/docs/pins/" class="text-primary hover:underline">pin caps</a>, <a href="/docs/needles/" class="text-primary hover:underline">needles</a>, <a href="/docs/tack-grammar/" class="text-primary hover:underline">tack</a> — takes a session or two to internalize. The <a href="/start/" class="text-primary hover:underline">five-minute start</a> covers the floor; the <a href="/docs/kernel-spec/" class="text-primary hover:underline">kernel spec</a> covers the ceiling.
          </div>
          <div class="font-body text-xs text-secondary">
            <strong class="text-on-surface">A daemon process.</strong> ostk runs a background daemon per project. Memory cost depends on what's loaded — a baseline daemon is tens of MB; with embeddings resident it can be hundreds. Use <code class="text-primary">ostk daemon restart</code> to swap a rebuilt binary in cleanly; <code class="text-primary">pkill -f "ostk daemon"</code> if you just want it gone.
          </div>
          <div class="font-body text-xs text-secondary">
            <strong class="text-on-surface">A directory you can't ignore.</strong> <code class="text-primary">.ostk/</code> is the substrate. Lose it and you lose the audit chain (the project still works — you just lose history). Back it up like you'd back up <code class="text-primary">.git/</code>.
          </div>
          <div class="font-body text-xs text-secondary">
            <strong class="text-on-surface">A small state footprint.</strong> Per-project state in <code class="text-primary">.ostk/</code>; per-machine state in <code class="text-primary">~/.local/share/ostk/</code> and <code class="text-primary">~/.cache/ostk/</code>. Nothing else.
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- CTA -->
      <section class="mb-8">
        <h2 class="section-label mb-4">NEXT</h2>
        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-px bg-surface-variant max-w-2xl">
          <a href="/start/" class="plausible-event-name=Why+to+Start bg-surface-container-high p-6 hover:bg-surface-container transition-colors">
            <div class="font-body text-sm text-primary font-bold mb-2">→ START_HERE</div>
            <p class="font-body text-xs text-secondary">Pick an onboarding path. Install the daemon or adopt components.</p>
          </a>
          <a href="/stack/" class="plausible-event-name=Stack+Overview bg-surface-container-high p-6 hover:bg-surface-container transition-colors">
            <div class="font-body text-sm text-primary font-bold mb-2">→ THE_STACK</div>
            <p class="font-body text-xs text-secondary">Review the decoupled components and licensing layout.</p>
          </a>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Doctrine pointer -->
      <p class="font-body text-xs text-secondary leading-relaxed max-w-xl mt-12 pt-6 border-t border-surface-variant">
        For the philosophy underneath these failures and the trajectory ahead, see <a href="/doctrine/" class="text-primary hover:underline">/doctrine/</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </main>
</Base>
